\subsection{Constructive Live-Handoff Certificate in the Riccati Metric}
\label{sec:iss-handoff-certificate}

Section~\ref{sec:iss-live-certificate} makes the linear part of the Live basin
constructive but leaves two obstructions.  First, its uniform-exponential
constants are stated in the fixed dimensionless coordinates of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-scales}, so the covariance contraction that the deployed
schedule actually exhibits has to be converted back into those coordinates
through $\sqrt{\bar p/\underline p}$; the diagnostic measures that conversion
factor as $555.7$ and the implied transition constant as $M_H\approx565.6$.
Second, the small-gain quantity $\nu_{\rm cert}$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-nu} prices every nonlinear injection at
$M_H/(1-\rho_H)$, which is the memory of the slowest mode in the estimator: at
the deployed \SI{5000}{s} residual-bias correlation time that is
$3.45\times10^{5}$ samples.  Multiplying the two makes the admissible radius of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-radius} numerically meaningless.

This subsection removes both obstructions by carrying the whole argument in the
Riccati metric instead of converting out of it, and by replacing the scalar
memory with per-channel injection gains.  It then constructs the handoff bounds
themselves, block by block, and states exactly which of them are proved, which
are checkable, and which are declared.

Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-computer-assisted-certificate} settles what an earlier draft
of this subsection had to leave as a standing interval hypothesis.  The two
questions are separable and are worth keeping separate.  Whether the constants
$1-\chi_H$, $\overline p$, $c_\xi$ and $r_\xi$ \emph{exist and are of the right
sign} over a declared compact domain is now proved there by verified
arithmetic, with no replay.  What their \emph{numerical values} are is a
different question, and the answer depends entirely on how the domain is
declared: the broad-box proof gives values that are strictly positive and
operationally useless, while the eight-point diagnostic measures values some
$290$ orders of magnitude tighter that are not a proof of anything about an
unexamined trajectory.  The certificate below carries both, labelled, and
refuses to let either be reported as the other.

\subsubsection{The metric and its two structural properties}
\label{sec:cert-metric}

Let $\mat P_k$ be the filter's covariance sequence on a Live interval.  It is a
deterministic sequence: the Riccati recursion depends on the schedule, the
Jacobians, and the accepted-update pattern, not on the measured values.  Define
the time-varying norm
\begin{equation}
\|\vct e\|_{V_k}:=\sqrt{\vct e^\top\mat P_k^{-1}\vct e}
=\|\mat L_k^{-1}\vct e\|_2,
\qquad
\mat P_k=\mat L_k\mat L_k^\top .
\label{eq:cert-metric-norm}
\end{equation}
Nothing here asserts that $\mat P_k$ bounds the true error.  It is used only as
a Lyapunov metric; every bound on an actual error in this subsection is
constructed in Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-handoff-bounds} from physical assumptions.

\begin{lemma}[Scale invariance]
\label{lem:cert-scale-invariance}
For any fixed nonsingular $\mat D$,
$\|\mat D\vct e\|_{(\mat D\mat P\mat D^\top)^{-1}}=\|\vct e\|_{\mat P^{-1}}$.
Consequently every induced norm of a transition, an injection, or a projection
taken between metrics of this family is invariant under the fixed coordinate
scaling of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-scales}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from
$(\mat D\vct e)^\top(\mat D\mat P\mat D^\top)^{-1}(\mat D\vct e)
=\vct e^\top\mat P^{-1}\vct e$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:cert-scale-invariance} settles a question the Phase-2
formulation had to argue rather than remove: the certificate below does not
depend on the choice of physical scales at all.  Equation~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-scales}
is retained for reporting, so that the Phase-2 columns of the diagnostic remain
comparable, and for nothing else.

\begin{lemma}[One-sample metric monotonicity]
\label{lem:cert-monotone}
Let $\mat A_k^{\rm cl}=(\Id-\mat K_k\mat C_k)\mat F_k$ be the closed-loop
transition of one accepted Kalman update executed in Joseph form, with
$\mat Q_k\succeq0$ and $\mat R_k\succeq0$.  Then
\begin{equation}
\alpha_k:=\bigl\|\mat L_{k+1}^{-1}\mat A_k^{\rm cl}\mat L_k\bigr\|_2\le1 .
\label{eq:cert-alpha}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $\mat P_{k+1|k}=\mat F_k\mat P_k\mat F_k^\top+\mat Q_k$ and let
$\mat N_k=\Id-\mat K_k\mat C_k$.  The Joseph form gives
$\mat P_{k+1}=\mat N_k\mat P_{k+1|k}\mat N_k^\top
+\mat K_k\mat R_k\mat K_k^\top$.  Hence
\begin{align}
\mat A_k^{\rm cl}\mat P_k(\mat A_k^{\rm cl})^\top
&=\mat N_k\bigl(\mat P_{k+1|k}-\mat Q_k\bigr)\mat N_k^\top\\
&=\mat P_{k+1}
 -\mat K_k\mat R_k\mat K_k^\top
 -\mat N_k\mat Q_k\mat N_k^\top
\;\preceq\;\mat P_{k+1}.
\end{align}
Congruence by $\mat L_{k+1}^{-1}$ preserves the ordering, so
$\mat L_{k+1}^{-1}\mat A_k^{\rm cl}\mat L_k
 (\mat L_{k+1}^{-1}\mat A_k^{\rm cl}\mat L_k)^\top\preceq\Id$, which is
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-alpha}.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:cert-monotone} is an identity of the deployed update, not a
hypothesis about the sea.  It is what removes the Phase-2 conversion factor:
the prefix quantity $B_H$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-prefix}, which had to be
measured in the fixed coordinates, is exactly $1$ in the metric.

\begin{theorem}[Metric finite-horizon UES certificate]
\label{thm:cert-metric-ues}
Suppose an uninterrupted Live interval satisfies, for one fixed horizon $H$,
\begin{equation}
\chi_H^V:=\sup_k\bigl\|\mat L_{k+H}^{-1}\Psi(k+H,k)\mat L_k\bigr\|_2
\le\bar\chi<1 .
\label{eq:cert-chiV}
\end{equation}
Then, with $\rho_H=\bar\chi^{1/H}$ and
\begin{equation}
\boxed{M_H=\rho_H^{-(H-1)},}
\label{eq:cert-MH}
\end{equation}
one has
$\|\mat L_k^{-1}\Psi(k,j)\mat L_j\|_2\le M_H\rho_H^{k-j}$ for all $k\ge j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Identical to Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-cert-finite-horizon}, with
$\bar B=1$ supplied by Lemma~\ref{lem:cert-monotone} and submultiplicativity
taken across the intermediate metrics, which is legitimate because the norms
compose: $\|\mat L_k^{-1}\Psi(k,i)\mat L_i\|
\|\mat L_i^{-1}\Psi(i,j)\mat L_j\|$ bounds
$\|\mat L_k^{-1}\Psi(k,j)\mat L_j\|$.
\end{proof}

At the eight committed reference operating points the diagnostic measures
$\alpha_k\le1$ at every sample, $\chi^V_{30}\in[0.9804,0.9825]$, and hence
$M_H\in[1.0179,1.0201]$: the constant the Phase-2 formulation had to take as
$565.6$ is $1.02$.

\subsubsection{Block-local bootstrap in the metric}
\label{sec:cert-bootstrap}

Let $\mat E_\xi,\mat E_\ell$ be the block selectors of
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-block-basin} and put
$\mat N_k:=(\mat E_\xi\mat P_k\mat E_\xi^\top)^{1/2}$,
$\mat\Lambda_k:=(\mat E_\ell\mat P_k\mat E_\ell^\top)^{1/2}$.  Define the two
block seminorms
\begin{equation}
\|\vct e\|_{\xi,k}:=\|\mat N_k^{-1}\mat E_\xi\vct e\|_2,
\qquad
\|\vct e\|_{\ell,k}:=\|\mat\Lambda_k^{-1}\mat E_\ell\vct e\|_2 .
\label{eq:cert-block-seminorms}
\end{equation}
Because $\mat N_k^{-1}\mat E_\xi\mat L_k$ is a coisometry,
$\|\vct e\|_{\xi,k}\le\|\vct e\|_{V_k}$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:cert-metric-ues} bounds the projected gains as well; write
$M_{\xi\xi}$ and $M_{\xi\ell}$ for the corresponding projected constants, both
of which may conservatively be taken equal to $M_H$.

The point of the seminorm is that membership of the tube converts directly into
per-block physical bounds.  If $\|\vct e_k\|_{\xi,k}\le R$ then, for each
sensitive block $b\in\{\theta,b_g,S,a_w,b_a\}$,
\begin{equation}
\|\delta\vct x_{b,k}\|
\le\sigma_{b,k}R,
\qquad
\sigma_{b,k}:=\bigl\|\mat E_b\mat N_k\bigr\|_2 ,
\label{eq:cert-block-sigma}
\end{equation}
which is the marginal standard deviation of that block.  This replaces the
fixed design scales of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-scales} inside the remainder
bound by the estimator's own interval envelopes, and it is the second place
where the metric formulation is quantitatively decisive: over the reference
interval $\sigma_S$ is between \SI{0.023}{m.s} and \SI{3.45}{m.s} against the
design scale $s_S=\SI{50}{m.s}$, and $\sigma_{a_w}$ between \SI{0.024}{m/s^2}
and \SI{0.053}{m/s^2} against $s_{aw}=\SI{6}{m/s^2}$.

Each nonlinear remainder enters the state through a known three-dimensional
channel: the group-composition remainder of the MEKF reset through the attitude
coordinates, and the measurement-model remainders of
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-acc-rem}--\eqref{eq:iss-cert-mag-rem} through the
corresponding Kalman gains.  Rather than pricing all of them at the scalar
memory $M_H/(1-\rho_H)$, define the directional $\ell_1$ injection gains
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_g
:=\sup_i\sum_{k>i}
 \bigl\|\Psi(k,i+1)\mat N_{g,i}\bigr\|_{V_k},
\qquad
\mat N_{g}\in\{\mat E_\theta^\top,\mat K_a,\mat K_m\},
\label{eq:cert-gamma}
\end{equation}
which are finite by Theorem~\ref{thm:cert-metric-ues} and are computed by
forward impulse propagation, with no matrix products to store.  The same
construction with $\mat N_g=\widetilde{\mat G}_k$ gives the disturbance gain
$\Gamma_d$ that multiplies the declared Live disturbance bound $\bar d$ below.  The scalar
bound is recovered by replacing every $\Gamma_g$ with
$M_H\|\mat N_g\|_{V}/(1-\rho_H)$, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-gamma} is never worse
and is dramatically better on the fast channels.

\begin{theorem}[Metric block-local entrance]
\label{thm:cert-metric-entrance}
Assume Theorem~\ref{thm:cert-metric-ues} on a Live interval beginning at $k_H$,
finite gains \eqref{eq:cert-gamma}, and the interval envelopes
$\sigma_b:=\sup_k\sigma_{b,k}$, $\|\mat E_\theta\mat K_u\|\le\varkappa_u$.
Define
\begin{align}
\hat c_\theta&=j_c\,\sigma_\theta
 \Bigl(\varkappa_a\bigl(f_1\sigma_\theta+\sigma_{a_w}+\sigma_{b_a}\bigr)
 +\varkappa_m m_1\sigma_\theta
 +\varkappa_S\sigma_S
 +h_{\max}\sigma_{b_g}\Bigr),\\
\hat c_a&=\tfrac12f_1\sigma_\theta^2+\sigma_\theta\sigma_{a_w},
\qquad
\hat c_m=\tfrac12m_1\sigma_\theta^2,
\label{eq:cert-chat}
\end{align}
and the small-gain slope
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
c_{\rm eff}
:=\Gamma_\theta\hat c_\theta+\Gamma_a\hat c_a+\Gamma_m\hat c_m .}
\label{eq:cert-ceff}
\end{equation}
Choose $0<r\le r_\xi$ with $\nu:=c_{\rm eff}r<1$.  If
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
M_{\xi\xi}\,\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\xi,k_H}
+M_{\xi\ell}\,\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\ell,k_H}
+\Gamma_d\bar d
<(1-\nu)\,r,}
\label{eq:cert-entrance}
\end{equation}
then $\|\vct e_k\|_{\xi,k}<r$ for every $k\ge k_H$ in the interval, and the
conclusion of Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} holds in the metric.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Project variation of constants onto the sensitive seminorm.  For as long as
$\|\vct e_i\|_{\xi,i}\le r$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-block-sigma} bounds every
physical block entering the remainders, so
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-cert-exp-remainder}--\eqref{eq:iss-cert-jinv} give
$\|\vct\eta_{a,i}\|\le\hat c_ar^2$, $\|\vct\eta_{m,i}\|\le\hat c_mr^2$ and an
attitude-channel remainder of magnitude at most $\hat c_\theta r^2$.  Summing
each channel against its own gain \eqref{eq:cert-gamma} bounds the nonlinear
convolution by $c_{\rm eff}r^2=\nu r$, and the strict inequality
\eqref{eq:cert-entrance} maps the closed tube into its interior.  Induction
closes the bootstrap.  Inside the tube the remainder is at most
$c_{\rm eff}r\|\vct e\|_{\xi}$, so the linear convolution gain is
$\nu<1$ and the usual discrete comparison gives an exponential rate strictly
below one together with a finite disturbance gain.
\end{proof}

Since the budget $(1-c_{\rm eff}r)r$ is maximised at $r=1/(2c_{\rm eff})$, the
certificate has one headline number: a handoff is admissible when
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
M_{\xi\xi}\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\xi,k_H}
+M_{\xi\ell}\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\ell,k_H}
+\Gamma_d\bar d
<\frac{1}{4c_{\rm eff}} .}
\label{eq:cert-budget}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Constructing the handoff bounds}
\label{sec:cert-handoff-bounds}

Equation~\eqref{eq:cert-entrance} needs a bound on the \emph{true} error of
each block at $k_H$.  The covariance cannot supply one.  What follows
constructs each bound and labels its provenance; the labels are carried in the
implementation as well, so a certificate can be read rather than trusted.

\paragraph{Attitude}
The deployed gate certifies the aligned branch
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch} and a sine residual
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-sine-gate}, giving
$\theta_{H,\rm tilt}\le\sin^{-1}(0.075)+\eta_g$ as in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}, and with an accepted magnetic gauge
$\overline\theta_H=\sin^{-1}(0.075)+\eta_g+\eta_m$.  Neither $\eta_g$ nor
$\eta_m$ is observable onboard.  A small accelerometer-direction residual is
the residual of a windowed average, and the part of the orbital specific force
that survives that window is precisely what $\eta_g$ has to cover; a
self-consistent learned magnetic reference likewise says nothing about absolute
heading, and $\eta_m=\sin^{-1}(\delta B_h/B_{h,0})$ is a declaration about the
reference error.  Both are therefore \emph{configured physical envelope}, and
the aligned-branch requirement is retained as a hard precondition rather than
absorbed into either.  Without a magnetic gauge there is no finite
$\overline\theta_H$ at all and the certificate refuses on those grounds.

\paragraph{Gyro bias}
The startup proxy carries an integral term whose stationary value is the
negation of the bias it cancels, so it is a candidate seed, and the coordinate
mapping is direct: the observer is driven in the same body frame as the filter,
and only the de-heel composition intervenes.  Only the two components
perpendicular to measured down are observable from a single vector.  Measured
against truth at handoff over the reference seas, however, seeding from it is
worse than not seeding: the integral also winds up against the horizontal
orbital acceleration, and the wind-up dominates.  The true gyro-bias error at
handoff is $1.6\times10^{-3}$ to $3.3\times10^{-3}$\,rad/s when the state is
seeded from the proxy, against $4\times10^{-5}$ to $1\times10^{-4}$\,rad/s when
it is left at zero, for turn-on draws of order $10^{-4}$\,rad/s.  The seed is
therefore implemented but disabled, and the bound remains the declared turn-on
envelope: \emph{configured physical envelope}.

\paragraph{World acceleration}
Initialising $a_w$ to zero declares the platform still, which in a seaway is
the one thing it is not.  The bootstrap can do better without any circular
dependency on the Live estimate: the filter's own measurement model is
$\vct f_b=\mat R_{wb}(\vct a_w-\vct g)$, so
$\vct a_{w,H}=\mat R_{bw}\vct f_b+\vct g$ evaluated in the proxy attitude is a
pure function of the measurements.  Its error is the levelling error times the
specific-force magnitude plus the accelerometer's own error,
\begin{equation}
B_{aw,H}=g\sin\overline\theta_H+\epsilon_{\rm acc}+B_{a,H},
\label{eq:cert-aw-bound}
\end{equation}
which is \emph{analytically constructed} from an envelope.  Measured against
truth the seed leaves \SI{0.04}{m/s^2} to \SI{0.51}{m/s^2} of error against a
constructed bound of \SI{1.95}{m/s^2}.

\paragraph{Integral state}
$S$ is the running integral of $p$, and its lower limit of integration is free:
$p$, $v$ and the $S=0$ pseudo-measurement model are all invariant under
$S\mapsto S+\text{const}$, because the pseudo-measurement asserts that the
displacement has zero running mean and shifting the epoch shifts the assertion
by the same constant.  Declaring the epoch to be the Live transition is
therefore a coordinate choice, not an estimate, and it makes
\begin{equation}
\boxed{B_{S,H}=0\quad\text{exactly}.}
\label{eq:cert-S-zero}
\end{equation}
The implementation performs the reset at handoff, zeroing the state, dropping
the $S$ cross-covariances that were learned against the discarded value, and
leaving a numerical floor in place of the constructor's \SI{50}{m.s}.  This is
\emph{exact by construction}.  It does not move $S$ out of the sensitive block:
$\delta S$ is zero only at $k_H$ and grows afterwards, and the
$S$~pseudo-measurement retains attitude rows through cross-covariance, so
Remark~\ref{rem:iss-block-phase1} stands.

\paragraph{Velocity and position}
These are kinematic-only after Phase~1 and enter \eqref{eq:cert-entrance} only
through $M_{\xi\ell}$.  A measurement-only translational bootstrap was
considered and then measured rather than built: with $v$ and $p$ entering Live
at zero, the true errors at handoff over the reference seas are
\SIrange{0.13}{3.33}{m/s} and \SIrange{0.04}{4.12}{m}, inside a declared
wave-orbital envelope that has to cover the \SI{8.5}{m} sea in any case, and
the term they contribute to the budget is smaller than the sensitive one.  The
bootstrap is therefore not implemented, and this paragraph is the measurement
that says so.  The bound is \emph{configured physical envelope}, and it scales
with the sea.

\paragraph{Accelerometer bias}
The deployed model is $b_a(T)=k_a(T-T_{\rm ref})+\beta_a$ with $\beta_a$ a slow
OU residual confined by the hard projection of
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-live-certificate}.  The projection bounds the
\emph{estimate}, not the true error; the true residual after temperature
correction is a calibration property and nothing onboard measures it.  It is
therefore \emph{configured physical envelope}.

\subsubsection{Coherence of the seed covariance}
\label{sec:cert-coherence}

Equation~\eqref{eq:cert-entrance} is evaluated in the metric of the covariance
the filter actually enters Live with, so that covariance and the constructed
bounds have to describe the same state of knowledge.  Phase~3 found that they
did not.  The constructor seeds the residual accelerometer bias at
\SI{0.004}{m/s^2} and re-synchronises $a_w$ to its OU stationary spread of
\SIrange{0.024}{0.053}{m/s^2}, while the declared calibration and levelling
envelopes bound the corresponding true errors at \SI{0.05}{m/s^2} and
\SI{1.95}{m/s^2}.  Entering Live an order of magnitude more confident than the
declared assumptions allow is overconfidence in the ordinary estimator sense,
and it also makes the entrance inequality report the disagreement rather than
the theorem.  The implementation therefore rewrites the seed marginals of the
envelope-bounded blocks to the constructed bounds themselves---one standard
deviation equal to a deterministic bound, which is the loosest reading of a
bound that is still defensible and is not a free parameter.

\subsubsection{Two sources for the same constants}
\label{sec:cert-sources}

Everything above is stated for a set of interval constants; nothing in it says
where they come from.  Two sources are available and they are not
interchangeable.

The \emph{analytical} source is
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-computer-assisted-certificate}.  Its lifted recursion
\eqref{eq:iss-ca-lifted-recursion} is stated at horizon boundaries, so
translating it into the form used here is immediate: metric monotonicity
(Lemma~\ref{lem:cert-monotone}) makes every intermediate step nonexpansive, so
$M_{\xi\xi}=M_{\xi\ell}=1$ exactly; invariance of $R\le r$ under
$R\mapsto\chi_HR+C_HR^2$ holds precisely when $C_Hr\le1-\chi_H$, so
\begin{equation}
 c_{\rm eff}=\frac{C_H}{1-\chi_H},
 \qquad
 r_\xi^{\rm metric}=\frac{r_\xi}{\sqrt{\overline p}} .
 \label{eq:cert-analytic-map}
\end{equation}
The verified values give
$c_{\rm eff}\le9.250114730570\times10^{288}$, whose reciprocal is exactly the
radius $R_{\rm cert}$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-ca-lifted-radius-num}.  A
certificate issued against these constants is a theorem.

The \emph{interval-measured} source is the diagnostic of
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-cert-diagnostic}, extended below.  Its constants are far
tighter and hold for the schedule that was replayed; nothing onboard verifies
that a running trajectory satisfies the same envelopes, so a certificate issued
against them is evidence about the deployed operating points and not a proof.
The implementation defaults to the analytical source, records on every
certificate which set decided it, and reports a pass under the measured set as
\emph{uncertified} with the reason \texttt{passed-unproved-constants}.

Because $M_{\xi\xi}=M_{\xi\ell}=1$ under both, the left-hand side of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-budget} does not depend on the source at all.  One evaluated
handoff can therefore be scored against both sets exactly, which is what the
validation of Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-validation} reports.

\subsubsection{Numerical evaluation, and the gap that remains}
\label{sec:cert-numbers}

Table-free summary of the diagnostic at the eight committed reference
operating points, with envelopes taken over a \SI{600}{s} established interval:
$\alpha_k\le1$ everywhere; $M_H\le1.0200$;
$\Gamma_\theta\in[1.23,1.35]\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{rad}^{-1}$;
$\Gamma_a\in[1.84,2.00]\times10^{3}\,(\mathrm{m/s^2})^{-1}$;
$\Gamma_m\in[2.73,3.93]\times10^{2}\,\mu\mathrm{T}^{-1}$.  The scalar
alternative $M_H/(1-\rho_H)$ is $3.45\times10^{5}$ samples, so the directional
gains are between one and three orders of magnitude tighter depending on the
channel.  The validity radius of the remainder expansion,
$r_\xi=\theta_c/(\sigma_\theta+A_g)$ with $\theta_c=1$, is between $88$ and
$327$, so it does not bind: the operating radius $1/(2c_{\rm eff})$ is at most
$0.74$.  The resulting slope is strongly sea-dependent:
$c_{\rm eff}=0.681$ at the \SI{0.27}{m} reference point and $29.28$ at the
\SI{8.5}{m} one, giving $r_{\rm cert}=1/c_{\rm eff}$ between $1.47$ and
$0.034$, and a budget $1/(4c_{\rm eff}M_H)$ between $0.360$ and $0.0084$.

Against that budget, the constructed handoff error in metric units is what the
seed covariance makes of the bounds of
Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-handoff-bounds}.  With the deployed seed and the default
declared envelope,
$\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\xi}=13.80$ and $\|\vct e_{k_H}\|_{\ell}=4.02$, so the left
side of Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-budget} is $17.82$ and the inequality would close
only for $c_{\rm eff}<0.0140$.  With the coherent seed of
Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-coherence} the same bounds give $2.74$ and $1.41$, a left
side of $4.16$, and $c_{\rm eff}<0.0601$.  Both numbers are regenerated by
\texttt{live\_entrance\_certificate-test} rather than quoted from a draft.

The certificate therefore does \emph{not} certify the deployed handoff under
either source, and the two failures are of completely different magnitudes.
Under the analytical source the admissible budget is
$2.70\times10^{-290}$ against a left side of $17.82$: short by some $291$ orders
of magnitude, which is the broad-box proof's own verdict on itself restated in
handoff coordinates.  Under the measured source the budget is
$8.53\times10^{-3}$: short by a factor of about $50$ at the calmest reference
point and about $2100$ at the roughest with the deployed seed, and by about
$12$ and $500$ with the coherent one.  The coherent seed is not enabled by
default: making it so moves the scored pitch RMS on one reference record from
inside its committed limit to outside it, and buying a factor that neither
source can use at the cost of a measured accuracy regression is not a trade
worth making.

That result is reported rather than tuned away, and it should be read against
where Phase~2 stood.  The transition constant alone has moved from $565.6$ to
$1.020$, a factor of $554$, and each injection channel is now charged its own
memory instead of the $3.45\times10^{5}$ samples the scalar form would apply to
all of them.  What is left is dominated
by two terms that are structural rather than incidental: $\Gamma_\theta$, the
$\sim\!\SI{100}{s}$ memory of the attitude channel, multiplied by the attitude
correction the $S$~pseudo-measurement and the magnetometer produce; and
$\Gamma_a\hat c_a$, the accelerometer residual remainder
$\|\delta\vct\theta\|\|\delta\vct a_w\|$.  Both grow with sea state, which is
why the calm and rough reference points differ by a factor of $43$.  Closing
the remaining gap requires either a structural change to the
$S$-to-attitude cross-gain---which is the change
Remark~\ref{rem:iss-block-phase1} already identifies as the one that would move
$S$ out of the sensitive block---or a sharper treatment of the quadratic
remainder than a single isotropic coefficient.  Neither is attempted here, and
neither is assumed.

\subsubsection{Theorem-certified and ordinary Live}
\label{sec:cert-state-machine}

The implementation keeps the two apart.  A Live interval is
\emph{theorem-certified} only when the startup proxy is ready, the aligned
branch holds, the magnetic gauge is available when the Live observability
hypothesis requires it, Eq.~\eqref{eq:cert-entrance} holds for the seed that
was installed, and the constants that decided it are the analytical ones of
Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-sources}.  A pass under the measured constants is recorded
on the certificate and reported as uncertified: it is a statement about the
eight reference operating points, and a running interval is not one of them
merely by resembling one.  Every other Live interval---including every
timeout-forced handoff, which by construction does not test the residual
magnitude---is \emph{uncertified}.  The distinction is exposed rather than
folded into a single flag, and nothing downstream may report an uncertified
interval as covered by Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-live}.

Certification is not permanent.  The hard maps the preceding sections already
treat as Live-interval boundaries---the $70^\circ$ tilt re-lock on either
branch, the second magnetic re-gauging, and any return to Cold---end the
interval the certificate was issued for, and the implementation clears the
certification when they occur.  Continuous re-certification during a Live
interval is deliberately not attempted: the entrance condition is what
Theorem~\ref{thm:cert-metric-entrance} needs, and a running check would have to
re-establish the interval envelopes, not merely the state.

Evaluating the certificate never withholds a handoff in the deployed
configuration.  A filter that refuses to start because a proof did not close
leaves a vessel with no estimate at all, which is the worse failure; the
optional gate exists for deployments that judge otherwise.

\subsubsection{Truth-based validation}
\label{sec:cert-validation}

Simulation has truth, so the safety property can be checked directly rather
than argued.  \texttt{live\_handoff\_validation.cpp} synthesises seas at the
eight committed reference $(H_s,T_p)$ pairs---synthesised rather than replayed,
because what is needed is exact truth for $v$, $p$, $a_w$, the body rate and
the attitude at one specific sample---and drives the deployed wrapper from cold
through $91$ scenarios: eight seas by three seeds by three wave phases with
gyro- and accelerometer-bias draws, plus large initial roll/pitch/yaw,
attitudes $170^\circ$ from level and so near but not on the antipodal set,
delayed magnetometer availability, sea state changing during startup, magnetic
reference errors at the edge of the declared envelope, and runs with no
magnetometer at all.  At every attempted handoff it records the true error of
each block, the bound that block was certified against, both sides of the basin
inequality, and the outcome.

The property the theorem claims is one-directional: if a handoff is certified,
every true handoff error must be inside the bound it was certified against.
Since the certificate currently certifies nothing, checking only certified
handoffs would be vacuous, so the test enforces the unconditional form
instead---on \emph{every} handoff, each finite constructed bound must bound the
true error.  Over the $91$ scenarios it does, with no violations and a worst
conservatism ratio of $1.07$; the two halves of the attitude bound are checked
apart, because their sum can hold while one of the two assumptions does not.
Two identities are checked separately: the integral state is exactly zero at
every handoff, and a handoff with no magnetic gauge is never certified.  Each
handoff is also scored against both constant sets of
Sec.~\ref{sec:cert-sources}, which is exact rather than a second run because
the left-hand side is source-independent.  All
$91$ scenarios reach Live, the latest at \SI{75}{s}, so the certificate does
not withhold the handoff; a further scenario turns the optional
\texttt{require\_certified\_live} gate on and is required not to reach Live,
so that the gate is known to gate rather than to do nothing.  The run also reports, for each scenario, the largest
$c_{\rm eff}$ that would have certified it, which turns a rejection into a
number and separates the part of the gap that belongs to the handoff from the
part that belongs to the interval.

The declared envelope in that test is wider than the library defaults, and
deliberately so: an envelope is a declaration, a deployment has to declare one
that covers what it will see, and declaring one that the scenario family then
leaves would be applying the theorem outside its own hypotheses rather than
testing it harder.  For the same reason the bias draws are uniform inside a
ball rather than Gaussian---a Gaussian leaves any finite envelope eventually,
and a failure for that reason would be the theorem correctly declining to cover
a case, not a defect in it.
